  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 25-year-old woman with idiopathic intracranial hypertension on acetazolamide presents with hyperpigmentation on her face after an allergic contact rash several weeks ago. What are the best treatments for her persistent hyperpigmentation?",
    "recommendations": "Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation from photosensitivity. First, daily broad-spectrum tinted sunscreen (SPF30+) with reapplication when outdoors is essential to prevent further darkening. If no evidence of active erythema or inflammation, can try lightening creams. For active lightening, prescribe hydroquinone 4% cream applied twice daily for up to three months (monitor for skin irritation and rare ochronosis; if irritation develops, discontinue and switch to an alternative depigmenting agent). As an over-the-counter option, a vitamin C serum can be used, noting it may trigger acne in some patients but is otherwise well tolerated. Azelaic acid is another option. Niacinamide 5% cream twice daily can also be added as an adjunctive depigmenting and barrier-supportive agent. Retinoids, whether topical or systemic, are considered contraindicated in IIH."
  },
